# A catalog constrains page composition

The v0 composes a featured-sites page from Page, Grid, SiteCard and Callout.
[Foldkit](./foldkit-web.svx) owns Model, Messages and browser navigation.

## Ownership

- `.brain/data/featured-sites.json` owns site facts. An editor is its only writer.
- `credit` keeps the provisional wording `Vojta (badass)`. Confirm it before treating attribution as final.
- `featuredSites` decodes that source. Its result supplies prerender Flags.
- Browser startup uses a disposable snapshot of the same source.
- Model holds the spec, featured-site replica and note visibility. Composition does not persist pages.

## The catalog boundary

`packages/core/src/page-catalog.ts` owns all product Zod definitions.
It uses `@json-render/core@0.21.0` and the existing exact `zod@4.6.5`.
No release-age exception is needed.

The core build derives ignored JSON hints and metadata from that catalog.
`generate-page-catalog.ts` writes them atomically. A bridge test catches stale hints.
No generated catalog artifact is committed.

The spec wire shape remains `{ root, elements }`.
`composePage` takes `{ spec: Schema.Unknown }`, with the upstream catalog JSON Schema hint on `spec`.
An explicit JSON wire codec preserves the hint through projections while its decoded type remains Unknown.
The handler lazily loads json-render validation and returns the decoded spec or typed repair paths.
The Flags and output codec checks wire structure and child references. It does not validate component props.
Native semantic validation runs in `composePage`, not in the wire codec.

The upstream multi-component catalog parser leaves props open.
Each selected component therefore runs its catalog Zod props parser.
A diagnostic pass accumulates props and graph failures even when another component has an unknown type.
Cycles and orphans fail composition. A page permits one rendered Callout outside repeat scopes.

Dynamic values receive syntax checks at composition and value checks against Model at render time.
SiteCard fields use native pointers such as `{ "$state": "/featuredSites/0/name" }`.
NUL and lone surrogate code units fail before Foldkit serialization.

## Upstream and host responsibilities

| Upstream core supplies | This bridge supplies |
| --- | --- |
| Catalog definitions, prompt generation and JSON Schema | The four-component product catalog and Effect failure mapping |
| Prop expression, visibility and repeat resolution | A pure Foldkit Html registry over the current Model |
| Action binding and parameter resolution | Named Messages and the host update function |
| JSONL stream compiler and patch helpers | Submission validation after compilation; no active stream transport |

Action parameters with `$item` resolve to an absolute state path.
Props with `$item` resolve to an item value.
Generated properties check this distinction.

The Worker keeps the native catalog in a cold module.
The entry-graph test keeps the unchanged 900,000-byte budget.
The build cache restores the ignored hints before source typechecking.
Alchemy builds SSR and client environments in a production child.
Asset prerendering runs only in the client environment.
The infrastructure test runs that exact child build and checks the Worker and featured asset.
An entry-graph test rejects eager Zod machinery. An eager-import mutation fails that test.

The adapter contains no product imports, StyleX, store, transport or effects.
The application owns its StyleX recipes and Toggle note Message.
Watchers, custom computed functions, confirmation flows and async action execution remain outside v0.
No JSON contains functions.

The stock upstream prompt describes optional providers that v0 does not install.
The tool description therefore publishes catalog-generated props and supported actions, not those stock instructions.
`catalog.prompt()` remains available. Streaming transport must configure its prompt template before adoption.

`no-zod-outside-catalog` permits only the named catalog boundary and the renderer package.
`no-renderer-product-imports` keeps application modules and StyleX outside the adapter.

## Replacement decision

More complicated locally, simpler overall.

| Braid in the prototype | Separation | Fence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Catalog definitions and a bespoke Effect decoder in `page-spec.ts` | Zod definitions stay in one boundary; upstream supplies parsing and metadata | Named Zod import boundary and generated repair properties |
| Product styles and switch dispatch in `render-spec.ts` | Styled registry in the app; interpretation in a framework package | Renderer product-import rule |
| A custom pointer slice for card data | Core resolves pointers against the current Model | Snapshot and repeat/action properties |

The deep seam is `createRenderer(catalog, components, messageForActions)(spec, model, h)`.
It deletes the custom component switch and pointer language.
The alternative was to keep a parallel engine and translate specs between both catalogs.
That would duplicate authority and repair rules.

Both designs have zero lifecycle states. Pure composition needs no machine or new service.
The existing capability Layer owns effects; the loader owns source access.
The cartridge-removal gate is not run: this is a renderer adapter, not an infrastructure cartridge.
Rollback removes the adapter composition and reverts the dependency commit. No stored pages need migration.
The material open question is whether v0 needs streaming transport. It currently proves completed native patches only.

## Port to slice A

- Keep the product catalog, build-derived hints, capability handlers and standalone renderer package.
- Carry the decoded loader result, spec and note visibility through Flags into Model.
- Register four styled components in the new shell.
- Map resolved actions to named Messages. Keep model changes in update.
- Keep build-only prerendering and the emitted `/featured/index.html` asset.
- Preserve MCP and code-mode registration at composition roots.
- Keep the focus token and single Callout accent.

[PROVENANCE.md](../../PROVENANCE.md) records the upstream adapter reference.
